#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060776} :activity/id #uuid "b70e2d1f-56a3-5e7e-b1dd-0d8ac4b754e0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043 :topic/id #uuid "7cda6484-cda1-5805-8a40-963bb1a60fdf" :topic/math-academy-id 204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060739 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060861 :tutorial/content "We can use reference angles to evaluate trigonometric ratios for angles outside the first quadrant. \n\nTo demonstrate, let's determine the value of $\\cos (135^\\circ),$ corresponding to the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ on the unit circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/be/bef50a070785e4bb86867ba6404942fd7eaa04b60720adfb04176bafe2fa2121.png)\n\nThe reference angle for $\\theta=135^\\circ$ is $\\theta_R=45^\\circ.$ Let's add this to our diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33b208e44aaa556a67ebaf7a6c1117b09adf863c56deb1bc1f5590e289062c4b.png)\n\nLet $P'$ be the image of $P$ under a reflection across the $y$-axis. This gives a pair of congruent angles of measure $45^\\circ,$ as shown.\n\n![](images/c4/c4875682498edaadc74a8af238fdf3293b4820025256df78e60867940061785e.png)\n\nAccording to the definition, $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ is the $x$-coordinate of the point $P.$ But this is the same as the $x$-coordinate of the point $P'$ *taken with the opposite sign.* \n\nThe $x$-coordinate of $P'$ equals the cosine of the reference angle. \n\n$$\n\\cos(45^\\circ)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\, \\underbrace{135^\\circ}_{\\theta} \\,) = - \\cos(\\, \\underbrace{45^\\circ}_{\\theta_R} \\,) = -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we simplified the evaluation of $\\cos(135^\\circ)$ by evaluating the cosine of the corresponding reference angle $\\theta_R=45^\\circ$ *with an additional sign adjustment* for the final result. \n\nWe can use a similar argument to show that, since $y = \\sin\\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin(135^\\circ) = \\sin(45^\\circ) = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 2}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we do **NOT** change the sign because the $y$-coordinates of $P'$ and $P$ *are equal.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c1461a2-d5b4-5d11-b0ad-6537ca726992" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10532 :tutorial/title "Calculating Trigonometric Ratios Using Reference Angles"} :step/id #uuid "bdeb7825-3a44-52de-9875-55a1ffbcd7e7" :step/math-academy-id 210844 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060778}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060776 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060971 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the cosine and sine ratios for *acute* angles can be defined in the following ways:\n\n- Using a right triangle. If $\\theta$ is an acute angle in a right triangle, then\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{adjacent}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = \\dfrac {\\text{opposite}} {\\text{hypotenuse}}.\n$$\n\n- Using the unit circle. If $\\theta$ is a central angle made by a point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle in the first quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y.\n$$\n\nWe can't define trigonometric ratios for non-acute angles using right triangles because a right triangle can't have angles larger than $90^\\circ.$ However, we can *extend* these ratios for non-acute angles using unit circles.\n\nTo do that, let's consider the unit circle with a central angle $\\theta$ in any quadrant, measured in the usual way.\n\n![](images/61/612d468ea23fefb6bbb33bd676a1930604b283475f36be08541419776bb580bd.png)\n\nWe *define* the sine and cosine of $\\theta,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = x, \\qquad \\sin\\theta = y\n$$\n\nIn other words, \n\n- the cosine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $x$-coordinate of $P,$ and\n\n- the sine of $\\theta$ is defined to be equal to the $y$-coordinate of $P.$\n\nThis way, our trigonometric ratios make sense for any angle, even if this angle is larger than $90^\\circ.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "67345ec4-5f02-5d0a-9900-417059fb1ba3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6762 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a1c1a6d-1eee-5229-b88d-9d28c1ae4c0a" :step/math-academy-id 40995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060777}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060777 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059830 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059389 :question/id #uuid "ec8644e2-c365-5dce-bb21-7a148203a03a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10818"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1810cacd-3565-5438-ae93-45b50970f5dd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542196}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Trigonometric Ratio Given a Point on the Unit Circle"} :step/id #uuid "c87cdc7f-1883-5d6b-8cb5-f78979ccbb35" :step/math-academy-id 40996 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060739}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060972 :tutorial/content "We can calculate a trigonometric ratio of *any* angle $\\theta$ by finding the ratio of the corresponding reference angle and adjusting the sign depending on which quadrant our angle lies in.\n\nTo do this, we perform the following steps:\n\n1\\. Find the reference angle $\\theta_R.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the ratio for $\\theta_R.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting ratio is positive or negative.\n\nWe must do the last step because the ratio for $\\theta_R$ will always be nonnegative, but the ratio for $\\theta$ may not be.\n\nBut how do we determine whether the ratio for $\\theta$ is positive or negative? This depends on the trigonometric ratio and which quadrant the angle is in.\n\nTo help us remember when various trigonometric ratios are positive, we use the mnemonic \"**A**ll **S**tudents **T**ake **C**alculus.\" \n\n- **A**ll of the trigonometric ratios are positive in quadrant I\n\n- **S**ine (and only sine) is positive in quadrant II\n\n- **T**angent (and only tangent) is positive in quadrant III\n\n- **C**osine (and only cosine) is positive in quadrant IV\n\nWe can represent this in a so-called CAST diagram, shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a6d43985058f1afd33272537def49886177aceab115f0084b1cf9d08ba78c43.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider $\\sin 140^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/da/da7ca1a89b91d157ad97a3800d8c2d67efb467a8ceaa8f3c964bea5275ecfc5d.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta = 140^\\circ$ is the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle $\\theta_R$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\sin{140^\\circ},$ and therefore we're interested in $\\sin\\theta_R = \\sin{40^\\circ}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\sin{140^\\circ}$ must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin 140^\\circ = \\sin{40^\\circ}.\n$$\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "807bb7a1-70c9-500c-be69-53b15b903672" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6763 :tutorial/title "The CAST Diagram"} :step/id #uuid "55b9e1e7-d450-5f66-8313-91113548a2fe" :step/math-academy-id 41000 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059390 :question/id #uuid "ea7f4d67-8d88-559f-b39e-7618d86f9379" :question/math-academy-id "e-10819"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41b43c0b-f0bb-54eb-9644-6db9398ab0a9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050795}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566262}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Sine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "b9889a1e-5c54-5abd-b023-829bf9ee0adc" :step/math-academy-id 41002 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059391 :question/id #uuid "926813b0-e2fb-5671-8b99-a00c4710c4d1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10820"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "669295c7-5c63-5926-907c-1e2c211c3570" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060330} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566255}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Cosine of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "99d08952-d027-5e02-b60d-55b149c7a7ca" :step/math-academy-id 41006 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060781}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060781 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059392 :question/id #uuid "0561b4af-2393-5bc1-99e0-9c996f13c4a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10822"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81fb8ad6-b9b7-5d2b-9ea5-17c6df4dea68" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542217} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566210}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Tangent of an Angle in Terms of a Reference Angle"} :step/id #uuid "6ead1064-5647-5f6e-a0fb-17f9f615df2b" :step/math-academy-id 41009}] :activity/title "Extending the Trigonometric Ratios Using the Unit Circle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059904}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566317} :activity/id #uuid "9139e18c-366b-55b6-82b3-e75c6b6d20b4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051043 :topic/id #uuid "7cda6484-cda1-5805-8a40-963bb1a60fdf" :topic/math-academy-id 204} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566290 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566210 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00b2ecca-75b6-58f7-9c07-3aae92dbe5b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565675} {:answer/id #uuid "20dae444-9a20-5951-872d-636ff74d0e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565732} {:answer/id #uuid "72107be3-b641-5537-86ee-9ce79f3a22d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565856} {:answer/id #uuid "adb24147-d3a9-5b6a-a6d7-62f15b0ad41f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡{130}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565953} {:answer/id #uuid "b428d1f0-e349-530d-a4b4-24f6a6274e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{130}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565966}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565856} :answer-field/id #uuid "44907bf6-096e-5980-bb96-b4f7f77f9980" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566134}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1bb75070-fc2e-5a03-b839-07558cdd2a36" :question/math-academy-id "q-156605" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${310}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/70/70325d2a1d2d252e20eea4f4940b8dc03b21c737709ffe65d95f36b51bb63fcf.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={310}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{310}^{∘} \\\\  & ={50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{310}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "52d91b10-95e2-5853-b058-9550461fe27f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566305 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566213 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19431c0a-bdd3-5225-b483-6b0891c69d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{163}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565721} {:answer/id #uuid "33c1b2bb-e8d5-5650-84a0-fe62bfe7ed4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{163}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565757} {:answer/id #uuid "7aab4335-e6f8-50ff-8426-deb0887acff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565869} {:answer/id #uuid "935f5752-b045-5b47-affc-7b3fa1825b85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{17}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565916} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bf9e37-3223-5311-ae58-de6d9f5ee716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566003}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566003} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d8fcf9e-df13-5b5c-a903-7cb43af2426c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27716467-fe24-5b2e-a5ee-4be993036021" :question/math-academy-id "q-156075" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${197}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/d4/d483d80833650a101f993d9f66ffb9c786681240a4bfa4e255fb116cc7ad5fd2.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={197}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={197}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={17}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{197}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{17}^{∘}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9ca7eb4-f756-5736-925f-230f1fbb54f7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566290}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e13521f-e2ed-5d00-b9ec-9e2f3dadbd85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565780} {:answer/id #uuid "63332c80-08c6-5406-95e6-80bf617f3929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{115}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565833} {:answer/id #uuid "6d610513-4b2e-5430-b55e-31a452283674" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565847} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e44820-bc6c-5c8d-9869-91c626a31610" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565991} {:answer/id #uuid "eadecdc5-c208-5546-bbf1-1d575e05ac4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{115}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566063}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565847} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d4c08d-a6b6-51ef-ab36-7ea7637d3cfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bd9a81c2-dd21-5cb3-9488-a8b270b5ad9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124446" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${245}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/31/312fc3581d9da413254a0eb4dbc64091b286675492821838ba9a65ee06f45a7c.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={245}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={245}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={65}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{245}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{65}^{∘}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f1616495-15a6-5d1e-979a-c28e0d2a8770" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566255 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a2505a0-14df-51c6-ad4c-3b9e3be1a49c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565744} {:answer/id #uuid "349f69bc-bcdc-5122-ac2c-a188f806b7b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565760} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8a5f81-69f6-532a-bfcc-6895c9a965a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565783} {:answer/id #uuid "5722dc1e-7879-5efa-9e3e-63d11d333004" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565819} {:answer/id #uuid "5b696c45-4f0f-51d6-a0a5-7c025c4830aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565828} :answer-field/id #uuid "290ee7a2-24f2-5c09-8d37-e125475616d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a1630114-3830-5e2d-b973-4d57fd87afe3" :question/math-academy-id "q-124455" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${140}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/5f/5f2b641a3a765f65f1d7d11837193341edb6876dfaea45e9eeaf1d7610178435.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={140}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f6120112-ac2e-5621-b0e7-28a667256cbb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566305}}] :activity/title "Extending the Trigonometric Ratios Using the Unit Circle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565669}]}
